
🔄 Hazelcast vs Redis

| Feature / Aspect            | **Hazelcast**                                                     | **Redis**                                                                |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Language Base**           | Java-based                                                        | C-based                                                                  |
| **Integration with Java**   | Native and seamless                                               | Good, but via external clients                                           |
| **Data Storage Type**       | In-memory **data grid**                                           | In-memory **key-value store**                                            |
| **Data Structures**         | Maps, Queues, Sets, Lists (Java-style)                            | Strings, Lists, Sets, Hashes, Sorted Sets, Streams, Bitmaps, HyperLogLog |
| **SQL Queries**             | ✅ Yes (Hazelcast SQL for querying maps)                           | ⚠️ Limited support via Redis Stack                                       |
| **Cluster Discovery**       | ✅ Built-in automatic node discovery (no setup required)           | ⚠️ Manual setup for Redis Cluster or Sentinel                            |
| **Persistence (Disk)**      | Optional, not core focus                                          | ✅ Yes (AOF, RDB) for durability                                          |
| **Pub/Sub Support**         | ✅ Yes                                                             | ✅ Yes                                                                    |
| **Spring Boot Integration** | ✅ Strong, with `hazelcast-spring` and native support              | ✅ Strong, with `spring-data-redis`                                       |
| **Use in Caching**          | ✅ Good for distributed, cluster-aware caching                     | ✅ Good for centralized or small-cluster caching                          |
| **Session Replication**     | ✅ Common in web apps for sticky sessions                          | ⚠️ Less common; requires setup                                           |
| **Performance**             | Fast, but slightly slower than Redis in basic key-value ops       | Extremely fast in basic caching (pure key-value)                         |
| **Scalability**             | Horizontal scaling with partitioned data grid                     | Horizontal scaling with sharding or Redis Cluster                        |
| **Best Use Cases**          | Java apps, distributed caching, clustering, session state sharing | Caching, messaging, leaderboard, queues, real-time analytics             |


🧠 Summary:
✅ Use Hazelcast if:

You're working in Java/Spring Boot

You want distributed cache, clustered architecture, or in-memory computing

You need SQL querying over in-memory objects

✅ Use Redis if:

You need ultra-fast caching, pub/sub, or temporary storage

Your application is language-agnostic

You require persistence/durability

You need advanced data structures (like sorted sets, streams)

🔧 Example Use Case Scenarios:
| Scenario                                              | Recommended Tool |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |
| Java app caching and cluster replication              | Hazelcast        |
| OTP storage or token expiry                           | Redis            |
| Leaderboard in gaming app                             | Redis            |
| Large enterprise Java system with shared memory state | Hazelcast        |
| Microservices event pub/sub                           | Both (depends)   |
